From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Fri, 26 Aug 1994 10:30:58 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-264] X-MEME: Anthropic Cosmological Principle

>>From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
>>Apropos of my comments about ACP and the existence of God, I see that Frank
>>Tipler has a new book out called _The Physics of Immortality_.  According
>>to the dust jacket blurb, Tipler feels that his Omega Point Theory not only
>>proves the existence of God, but implies the corporeal resurrection at the
>>End of Time of everyone who has ever lived.

 I haven't seen that book, but from what I have read about this idea (mainly
in his article in Zygon vol. 24 (1989), "The Omega Point as Eschaton", which
we discussed on this list in October 1992), it is a reasonable theory, but
it will be quite a while before we have any basis for deciding whether or
not it is true. So the claim that it "proves" something sounds ridiculous.
 The basic idea is that the universe will support an infinite amount of
information processing, which implies that the Omega Point (the hypothetical
point at which the universe converges in the infinite future) will have
simulated all possible phenomena. This includes a simulation of all possible
lives of all possible beings (possibly excluding undesirable lives? (boring
lives? tortured lives?) Tipler conjectures that the Omega Point will be
benevolent like other "gods"). Whenever this simulation happens to continue
the life of someone who previously existed, it amounts to resurrection.
 The Omega Point seems to have most of the qualities of a typical deity
(including omniscience, omnipotence). There is no obvious way to determine
whether it cares if we worship it or expects us to follow an arbitrary
set of rules it has given to our ancestors.

